---
import ArticleLink from '@/components/ArticleLink.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import Home from '@/layouts/Home.astro';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';

const items = await getCollection('news');

items.sort((a, b) => {
    return a.id < b.id ? 1 : -1;
});

const {
    id,
    data: { title, date, abstract, tags },
} = items[0];
---

<Home>
    <p style="font-size: var(--huge); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        We are Trope&nbsp;Informed.
    </p>
    <p>
        Here is the introductory paragraph about Trope Informed. It includes
        lots of information about the organisation and what they do. That's
        really helpful for people who visit the site.
    </p>
    <p>Here's a second paragraph, for style testing purposes.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Here are some bullet points about what they do.</li>
        <li>Many of the things they do are very important.</li>
        <li>
            Some of them are more complex and require breaking down into more
            detailed points:
            <ul>
                <li>For example, this point.</li>
                <li>Also, this is a thing.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>This is an extra item just to validate margin behaviour.</li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Others are not necessarily more complex, but require more
                explanation. For those instances we might need to put in
                paragraphs.
            </p>
            <p>How do those paragraphs look? Well, they look like this.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        That's all well and good, but what if we need to imply an order to these
        items? Well, we could restate the same thing, using a numbered list
        rather than bullet points. Here's how that would look:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>Here are some bullet points about what they do.</li>
        <li>Many of the things they do are very important.</li>
        <li>
            Some of them are more complex and require breaking down into more
            detailed points:
            <ol>
                <li>For example, this point.</li>
                <li>Also, this is a thing.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
        <li>This is an extra item just to validate margin behaviour.</li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Others are not necessarily more complex, but require more
                explanation. For those instances we might need to put in
                paragraphs.
            </p>
            <p>How do those paragraphs look? Well, they look like this.</p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    <Fragment slot={'sections'}>
        <Section
            heading="Latest news"
            cta={{
                text: 'All news',
                url: '/news/',
            }}
            color="cerulean"
        >
            <ArticleLink
                date={date}
                title={title}
                description={abstract}
                permalink={`./${id}/`}
                tags={tags}
            />
        </Section>
    </Fragment>
</Home>
